<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ValidationRule xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        ValidationRule.description is capped at 255 characters. The rationale below is
        operationally important, so it lives here rather than being trimmed; a comment is
        repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata API. It is INSIDE the root element on
        purpose - a comment ABOVE <ValidationRule> breaks `sf` deploy at conversion with
        the misleading error "unable to find matching parent xml file". Same shape as the
        two incumbent Lead rules in this folder.

        WHAT IT DOES. Blocks a Lead from ENTERING 'Under Review' unless Deal Type and
        Listing Status are BOTH filled. Spec sec 2.4 / requirements sec 3.

        ============================================================================
        IT ENFORCES TWO FACTS, NOT THREE. THE PROPERTY ADDRESS LEG WAS DELIBERATELY
        DROPPED - IT IS ENFORCED BY THE INCUMBENT RULE IN THIS SAME FOLDER.
        ============================================================================
        USER DECISION, 2026-08-14. The spec's sec 2.4 defines this as a THREE-fact gate
        (Deal Type + Listing Status + Property Address). The third fact is already
        enforced, on the identical transition, by the live sibling rule

            Property_And_Email_Required_To_Progress

        whose formula is ISCHANGED(Status) + ISPICKVAL(Status,'Under Review') OR
        ISPICKVAL(Status,'Qualified'), blocking on ISBLANK(Property_Address__c) OR
        ISBLANK(Email). Salesforce evaluates EVERY active validation rule on a save and
        surfaces EVERY failure, so a Lead missing only the address tripped BOTH rules and
        Lightning showed TWO error messages for ONE missing field. Two messages for one
        cause is not extra safety - it is noise that trains an analyst to stop reading
        them.

        The address requirement is therefore NOT forgotten and MUST NOT be re-added here.
        If you are reading this because a Lead reached 'Under Review' with a blank
        address, the rule to look at is Property_And_Email_Required_To_Progress, not this
        one. Note that the incumbent is the STRICTER of the two on address: it gates
        'Qualified' as well as 'Under Review'.

        THE INCUMBENT WAS NOT TOUCHED, deliberately - it is a live rule with production
        history and its own Email leg, and editing it is a separate change with its own
        blast radius.

        THE OUTCOME_NO_ADDRESS EXPLANATION MOVED TO THE INCUMBENT. CLOSED 2026-08-14,
        USER DECISION. Spec sec 2.4 required that the blocking message NAME the
        addressless-lead case so it reads as the intended chase rather than a bug - see
        below for why that matters. This rule cannot carry that clause: it no longer fires
        on a blank address, and a message describing a condition the rule does not test is
        worse than no message. So the clause now lives in

            Property_And_Email_Required_To_Progress

        whose <errorMessage> was extended to say that some leads legitimately arrive with
        no address and that supplying one is the intended next step. ONLY that rule's
        message changed - its formula, name and every other element are untouched, so it
        still gates BOTH 'Under Review' AND 'Qualified' and remains the stricter of the
        two on Property_Address__c. That file's comment points back at this one; the split
        is discoverable from either side.

        ============================================================================
        WHY THE ADDRESSLESS POPULATION NEEDS EXPLAINING AT ALL (retained context).
        ============================================================================
        Branch (c) of the routing tree (outcome label 'New Lead (property, no address)',
        OUTCOME_NO_ADDRESS) deliberately holds Property_Address__c NULL: the email named a
        property the pipeline could not address, so `claimableAddress()` refuses to store a
        value that could not have produced a claim key. Those Leads are LEGITIMATE and are
        SUPPOSED to be chased by a human - they have no first-broker-wins protection until
        someone supplies the address. A blocked save on such a Lead is the system working,
        not a pipeline defect.

        ============================================================================
        ASSERTED, NOT ASSUMED: THIS RULE CANNOT FIRE ON THE INBOUND PIPELINE'S OWN LEAD
        INSERT. Three independent guarantees, any one of which is sufficient.
        ============================================================================

        (1) STRUCTURAL. ISCHANGED() evaluates to FALSE in a validation rule on record
            CREATION. It is the first term of the top-level AND(), so this rule is
            unreachable on ANY insert - not only the pipeline's.

        (2) EMPIRICAL - THE VALUE. Both Lead-creating paths in this application write
            Status = 'New', never 'Under Review':
              - EmailToLeadService.cls (constant STATUS_NEW = 'New') - the inbound
                broker-email pipeline, branches (c) and (e);
              - BrokerPortalService.cls - the public guest portal.
            Verified 2026-08-14 by a repo-wide grep for Lead Status writes; those are the
            only two non-test sites, and no Apex class anywhere writes 'Under Review'.
            So even without (1), the ISPICKVAL leg is false at insert. Under the staging
            model the Lead is created ONCE, COMPLETE (ARCHITECTURE.md sec 1), so there is
            no follow-up pipeline update to catch it either.

        (3) PRECEDENT - ALREADY PROVEN IN PRODUCTION. The incumbent sibling
            Property_And_Email_Required_To_Progress uses the IDENTICAL
            ISCHANGED(Status) + ISPICKVAL(Status,'Under Review') shape and has been live
            through the pipeline's entire production history. Its own comment records the
            branch-(c) population as unable to PROGRESS - never as unable to be CREATED.

        The only writers of 'Under Review' are the leadMarkUnderReview headless quick
        action (LDS updateRecord) and a manual edit. Both are deliberate human acts, which
        is exactly the population this gate is for.

        ============================================================================
        RESIDUAL, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: 'Qualified' IS NOT GATED BY THIS RULE.
        ============================================================================
        The spec says "entry to Under Review", so that is what this enforces. The UI path
        is New -> Under Review -> Qualified (leadMarkQualified is shown only on the 'Under
        Review' stage by a Dynamic Actions visibility rule), so in practice a Lead reaches
        Qualified through this gate. But a direct API write or a manual edit can go New ->
        Qualified and bypass it. The incumbent DOES gate Qualified for its own two fields,
        so there is in-repo precedent for adding it here if that hole matters. Not added
        without a decision.

        ============================================================================
        FORMULA NOTES.
        ============================================================================
        - ISBLANK(TEXT(picklist)) is the blank test for BOTH referenced fields. It
          composes two standing repo rules: ISBLANK over ISNULL, and TEXT() as the
          picklist accessor (see Completed_LOI_Before_PSA and No_Backward_Stage_Movement,
          which record why). Both Deal_Type__c and Listing_Status__c are picklists, so
          after the address leg was dropped this rule has NO direct-ISBLANK term left -
          that is expected, not an omission.
        - NOT(IsConverted) mirrors the incumbent's defensive guard. Database.convertLead
          DOES change Status (to 'Converted'), so ISCHANGED(Status) is true at conversion;
          the ISPICKVAL leg already excludes it structurally, but the guard exists so a
          future edit adding a status to this rule cannot accidentally start blocking
          conversions.
        - errorDisplayField is Status - the field being changed, and the only one of the
          three referenced fields guaranteed present on both the layout and the Path.
          Attaching to either required field would highlight the wrong field whenever the
          other one is the blank one.
        - ISCHANGED-scoping means Leads ALREADY sitting in 'Under Review' are not frozen.
          Same known-open insert-time hole the incumbent accepts, and accepted here for
          the same reason.
        - Listing_Status__c is created in this same pack. A Metadata-API-deployed custom
          field arrives with NO field permissions for ANY profile, but a validation rule
          reads the field in SYSTEM context, so this rule works regardless of FLS - the
          user simply cannot SEE the field they are being asked to fill. That is what
          post-deploy gate G3 (assign the permission sets) exists to prevent.
    -->
    <fullName>Deal_Facts_Required_For_Under_Review</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>Blocks a Lead from entering Under Review unless Deal Type and Listing Status are both filled. Property Address is deliberately NOT tested here - the incumbent rule Property_And_Email_Required_To_Progress already enforces it. See this file&apos;s XML comment.</description>
    <errorConditionFormula>AND(
    ISCHANGED(Status),
    NOT(IsConverted),
    ISPICKVAL(Status, &apos;Under Review&apos;),
    OR(
        ISBLANK(TEXT(Deal_Type__c)),
        ISBLANK(TEXT(Listing_Status__c))
    )
)</errorConditionFormula>
    <errorDisplayField>Status</errorDisplayField>
    <errorMessage>Deal Type and Listing Status are both required before this lead can move to Under Review. Fill them in on the lead first.</errorMessage>
</ValidationRule>
